In a (no longer earth-shattering record-breaking) period of time of
slightly less than four months, I've honed my skill to the point where
I have (at long last) ascended my first character, after being nagged
interminably by Eidolos and the gang on #nethack and continuing to
tell them "I'll get around to it someday" and burying myself in my
patching and experimenting.  :)

She started out as many of my other NAO characters.  I'm always one to play
elves, and after a streak of not-particularly-serious games with wizards
I decided to experiment with rangers.  Stratocaster (pet dog) and
SGrunt entered the dungeon uneventfully and proceeded to start the normal
strategy of Minetown -> Sokoban -> Mines' End that has appealed to me.

Things started to get interesting as SGrunt fought off a few gnomes in
Minetown.  As you'd expect from many a YASD horror story, SGrunt managed
to hit a watchman with not one but two stray arrows, summoning the watch.
In a moment of panic, she quickly dove into the nearby temple... which
was cross-aligned.  Not one to succumb to her fear she stood her ground
and managed to skewer the entire Minetown watch - including the watch
captain - in very short order.  (The helmet taken from the watch captain
would last her through most of the game; its demise will be touched on
later.)

On the way back out SGrunt located a gray stone which she was able to
kick around.  She quickly took it back to Minetown and determined by
means of the shopkeepers that it was a luckstone; the plans to proceed
to Mines' End were put on hold.

Heading back into the dungeon and solving Sokoban yieled an amulet of
reflection.  SGrunt did not mind completely, as the Longbow was obviously
not going to suffice in all circumstances; she did need to unload some
gear in Sokoban on the way back out, as the burden was getting to her.

By this time SGrunt had two pet large cats and a warhorse; Stratocaster
succumbed after being left outside of Sokoban and becoming untame (but
still peaceful) and falling victim to Telecaster II (one of the large cats).
With them in tow a leprechaun hall on the fourteenth level of the
dungeon was cleared out, and a large amount of gold was brought back to
the priest in Minetown.

On the way back, a previously unnoticed altar was found on dungeon level
four.  It was swiftly converted to the will of Mars, though would not
otherwise prove much use.

SGrunt was about experience level 11 at the time and thinking about going
on the Quest.  Forgetting about Mines' End for the moment, she decided
the time was ripe to go pay a visit to the castle she had heard about.
Telecaster the first and Precision, the warhorse, would die on the
way down, falling victim to Telecaster II after having been left behind.
(SGrunt was noever very good at keeping pets with her.)

A throne on dungeon level 16 was able to tell her - to her delight -
that the two rings she had been wearing since the start of the game
were those of free action and slow digestion, and that a third ring
of hers was a ring of conflict.

On the following level, Telecaster II would uncover a previously unseen
helmet and retrieve it.  SGrunt, now level 12 and confident in the helm's
uncursed status, quickly swapped it for the watch captain's helmet.

"Your mind oscillates briefly."

SGrunt was furious as the protection of Mars left her; fortunately she
was still well protected by her original cloak and a mithril-coat she
had found in the mines.  She would nevertheless hold onto the helmet
after managing to remove it at some length.

In the process, some trolls decided to harass her.  Getting impaitent -
and with the knowledge that she had the power to make monsters fight
each other instead of attacking her - she pulled out her pick-axe and
decided to make some more progress.  Two uneventful digs landed her in
a temple of Venus two floors below her, and a nearby fountain provided
the means to remove the now-cursed helm of opposite alignment.  Afte
checking to ensure she did not have enough gold to consider talking to
the priest in the temple, she proceeded down.

Dungeon level 24 would land her on the opposite side of a long room
from an angry-looking titan.  She hoped the titan would not notice her,
and attemped to continue to dig downwards.  Two boulders were
hurled her way which she managed to evade, and she managed to weave
her way around the titan as he closed to killing distance.  A
third boulder provided the cover she needed to progress further down.

Four levels below she strode up to the massive gates of the castle, the
eels and sharks in the moat squirming furiously as they attempted to
tear each other to shreds.  Retrieveing a tooled horn from a normal bag
- both of which had been found in Minetown - she proceeded to open the
gate to the castle and use its power to crush its inhabitants.  With the
help of a potion she had borrowed from a nymph, she was easily able to
locate the mystic wand in the castle.

With the knowledge that the wand's powers of creation were limited, she
first wished for the requisite scrolls that would be needed to power it.
She was disappointed to only recieve one instead of the requested two,
but quickly realised she had seen this scroll before and was in fact
carrying one with her (though not in the blessed state to be able to
recharge the wand to its full potential).

Her instincts then kicked in, and the second wish was for a suit of
silver dragon scale mail so as to be able to let her wear another
amulet as she would like to be able to do at some point.

Another attempt to use the wand revealed it was empty.  She quickly made
use of the scroll she had first wished for to restore its power.

Following this she quickly obtained a magic-resistant cloak, a pair
of boots of speed, and a pair of gauntlets that enabled her to heft
the large load she was carrying.

She was aware that she could still obtain one more object, turning
the wand to dust, and spent some time contemplating what would be
her last request.  Eventually, she decided to generate that magical
bag capable of reducing the weight of it inside.

All of her wished-for items would last for the balace of her dungeon
crawl (so to speak).

With the castle looted of its most valuable possession, and most of
its inhabitants dead, SGrunt was still not experienced enough to be
able to go on the quest to retrieve the Longbow of Diana and the
Bell of Opening.  She finally decided to attempt to head back to
Mines' End and see what experiences might lurk there.

Four flights of stairs up, she beheld a strange beast with hair made
of snakes and a statue of a knight.  She realised this must be Medusa,
and that Medusa was asleep.  A swift kick awakened the beast; her
armour appeared to protect her from Medusa's fatal gaze, but she
did not understand why the beast did not turn to stone as a result.
She glanced down at her hand and realised she could see through
herself - she must be invisible to Medusa!  She quickly wrapped herself
with the wrapping of some long-forgotten mummy and the power of her
dragon scale armour was quickly responsible for the creation of
a new statue.

The statue of the unfortunate knight yielded a polished silver shield
and an ominous-looking pair of boots.  Realising these boots must have
been how he had arrived, she unthinkingly put on the boots to make her
escape - and escape she did, though not without the titan on the opposite
side of the lake screaming furiously and summoning a large number of
minions who mostly tore each other apart due to the power of SGrunt's
ring.

Even further on her ascent she was taken aback to find Telecaster II,
who still recognised her.  They quickly continued back on their journey
to Mines' End.  The balance of the journey to Minetown was mercifully
uneventful.

Three levels below Minetown SGrunt ducked into a narrow passage, with
Telecaster II leashed to her and following closely behind.  Unthinkingly
she stepped onto an apparently innocous patch of earth and felt oddly
different for a moment.  As she mindlessly continued walking she
looked behind her to suddenly find her leash attached to a small dragon,
who looked down at her peacefully; she realised she had just found a
polymorph trap.  As she attempted to walk around it Telecaster II would
change forms several more times until her leash was attached to a fearsome
Jabberwock.  On a whim from some verse she had heard written about the
beast, she decided to call him instead the Frumious Bandersnatch.

SGrunt and the Bandersnatch made their way uneventfully through the
Gnome King's Wine Cellar and retrieved the luckstone there.  On the
way back up, the Bandersnatch ducked into the temple where SGrunt's
first batch of divine protection had been obtained.  SGrunt followed
to behold the body of the priest, and her fortune on the priest's body;
this pleased her as her previous protection had been lost to the helm
of opposite alignment (which she still had, stowed safely in her bag
of holding).

Inspired, she took the Bandersnatch around Minetown to clear out some
of the creatures that lurked there.  The bodies were swiftly carted
and offered to Mars on the altar, which Venus was unable to protect.
On the last stroke, a surprise came to her as an ominous black blade
appeared at her feet, with a voice booming "Use my gift wisely!".
SGrunt recognised this as the fabled Stormbringer, and eyed her
Bandersnatch cautiously; if she was inattentive Stormbringer would
cause the Bandersnatch to meet its fate.

Still having failed to achieve the requisite experience to go on the
Quest, SGrunt decided to head back to the castle, below which a valley
was reputed to lurk that contained creatures whose spectral corpses would
make an interesting experience of a meal.

To her surprise, she encountered a wraith long before she was able to
reach the castle again; she had to restrain the Bandersnatch from 
consuming its shadowy remains.  Unfortunately, in the process, the
black blade at last got its way and fell the fearsome beast. 
Undeterred, SGrunt drew a wand from her bag and pointed it at the corpse,
which miraculously revived - but there was a look of pure hatred in
the Bandersnatch's eyes now, and she was sadly forced to slay the
beast once again.

SGrunt realised that if she was to find even one more such creature
and devour its corpse, she would prepared to go on the Quest; to
that end she proceeded on.

She stepped down the stairs back into the titan's lair, fully expecting
a furious fight.  Unfortunately, she failed to take into account the
beasts he had summoned; even as the giant was felled by one of its own
fire elementals a fearsome tentacled beast was able to get the jump on
her, and she suddenly lost her memory of much of the previously explored
dungeon.  Undeterred, she was able to flee from the beast and slay it
with a stack of daggers (her arrows had long since run out).

In the process, she witnessed the slaying of a fearsome beast she
recognised as a cockatrice (despite her amnesia).  She had heard of
its powers, and desired to harness them; she cautiously handled the
corpse and hefted it to serve as a weapon, and promptly stepped into
the same hole she had dug the first time she was there.

A minotaur promptly accosted her; she swung the cockatrice corpse by
its tail and hit the beast, which promptly turned into a statue.  She
grinned devilishly, feeling she would make further use of this as
opportunities presented themselves.

Eventually she reached the castle once more, and to her surprise she was
accosted by another titan.  Despite managing to summon some pets he,
too, was turned into a statue.  Shortly afterwards the cockatrice's
decomposed body decayed completely, to her disappointment.

Stepping behind the site where the castle's throne once stood, she
uncovered a hidden door and entered the corridor.  She had not stepped
very far before a trap door opened beneath her feet, and she plummeted,
landing in the infamous Valley of the Dead.  She did not get very far
before being beset by additional wraith.

SGrunt attempted to avoid destroying the wraith, as she knew that their
remains would not likely appear in this environment.  To her surprise,
however, one she was forced to kill did indeed leave a corpse.  One
quick meal later she felt ready to go speak with Orion.

The Quest was as uneventful as the journey back to its portal (situated
in a distinctly primitive-looking area of the dungeon); it was not much
later that Scorpious fell to Stormbringer and the Longbow - and the
Bell of Opening - were in SGrunt's grasp.

SGrunt thought at the time that she did not feel protected enough to
attempt to proceed past the Valley, but a search of her bag eventually
turned up some scrolls which she remembering having a positive effect
on armour.  Still not satisfied, she began to search the previously
explored areas of the dungeon.

Her amnesia kicked in, but fortunately she was in a state where she
could explore virtually at leisure.  During this time, she also had
a feeling that she would need to be able to make it between pairs
of stairs very quickly and began to dig passages between them.  In
the process, she fell what she recognised as a tengu, which left a
body.  In one of her most sinister errors, she proceeded to make a
meal of it - which momentarily caused her to be flung halfway across
the current dungeon level, seemingly at random.

This teleportitis would haunt her throughout the rest of the game.
She never saw another tengu that left a body.

Desperate, she realised that there were some areas where teleportation
was prevented by some mysterious force, and had a feeling that a greater
number of them were below the Castle.  A hurried descent led her to
the gates of Gehennom, which she entered without hesitation.

By this time, a meal of fire giant had granted a mild chill, as well
as other creatures granting her a variety of resistances.  She felt
as prepared as she was going to get descending further.

Not long afterwards she was beset by Asmodeus, who attacked her at once.
She realised this was because all of her money was in her bag, and
decided to keep that in mind.  She chased Asmodeus back to the stairs and
stood there, blocking his escape; it was not long before he succumbed
to Stormbringer.

Juiblex fell in one blow after a harrowing experience where she had
been swallowed by the beast; fortunately she had been prepared and
mortally wounded him with a well-placed blow from within by a wand
she had seen others use to dig holes.

As she approached the gate to Baalzebub's lair, fully intending on
killing the prince, a fell skeletal creature beset here and began
flinging spells.  Baalzebub attempted to capitalise on the situation,
but SGrunt had been fortunate to have two gold pieces on her person
this time.  Too distracted by battling the master lich (as it turned
out to be) she flung one of them at Baalzebub, who promptly disappeared.

The master lich was soon put down, and SGrunt soon found herself near
the outskirs of a bizarre corruption of a town.  She recognised
this as the hiding place of Orcus, and grimaced as she remembered
the death-dealing wand he was rumoured to possess.

The thought of hitting him with a cockatrice corpse occured to SGrunt,
and she grinned as she unslung her pickaxe and dug down, intent
on assailing Orcus from below.

After an incident where she could not find the stairs and used gold
pieces as bread crumbs to track where she had been, the stairs up
to Orcus' lair turned up, with a cockatrice following soon after.
She quickly slew the creature and hefted its corpse before fleeing
upstairs, and soon was to see a status of Orcus.

While exploring Orcus-town, a magic marker and a magic lamp turned up.
She seized upon them, intent on using their powers to enhance her
armour, and stashed them so as to find a location to accrue blank
scrolls upon which to write.

Another stairs up from Orcus-town were located, and SGrunt soon found
herself in the tower of Vlad the Impaler.  By then she had already
found a precious medallion within which was contained the power of
life, and was pleased to find another within the tower.  She beheld
a pair of boots in another closet which she new from the rumours of
Vlad's Tower to grant the power to walk on water, which she knew
she could use to blank scrolls.

Tales of Vlad's lack of strength had spread to her ears, and she felt
compelled to play up on them.  She had found a tin opener in the
dungeon, and had deliberately let it fall victim several times to
a rust trap; the intent was to see if Vlad could fall to the power
of such an insignificant object.

Needless to say, he did.  The tin opener was subsequently declared
Vladsbane.

The candelabrum he was rumoured to hold was pried from his ashes,
and SGrunt quickly hurried back to the swamp where Juiblex had
recently resided.  With the aid of Vlad's boots and her stash
of unused scrolls, she soon was able to improve her armour to
the point where she felt she could take on even the Wizard of
Yendor.  Stormbringer gleamed menacingly with the menace of its
newfound power, and even the Longbow of Diana found some room
for improvement.

It was not long after that SGrunt found at a point deep in the
depths of Gehennom a location where the very ground vibrated
underneath her feet.  The candelabrum seemed to be attracted to this
location; she left it there along with the Bell and made a decorative
pattern around them with some spare gold of hers.

She soon found the entrance to the Wizard's Tower, and battled her way
through two levels.  It was at this point that she decided to assess
what equipment she would keep with her during the final battles.  She
felt compelled to determine what her remaining scrolls were, knowing
that she would need the power to detect gold at some point in the future.

It was here that the watch captain's helmet finally was destroyed,
falling victim to one of the scrolls SGrunt carried.  She felt foolish
about this knowing that the power of the scroll could easily have
targeted one of her other more useful items of armour.  Nevertheless
she soond found she had two of a scroll, one of which when read
pointed her to the large stash of gold over in the room she had just
fought through.  With her second magic marker (obtained from the
djinni in the lamp) in hand she wrote several of these scrolls.

With Orcus' wand to hand, SGrunt fought her way towards the hiding place
of the Wizard of Yendor, who was soon awakened.  She proceeded to miss
not once but four times with the wand before the fifth caused his body
to plunge into the moat surrounding him, taking the Longbow of Diana
with him.  Undeterred a wand of cold was found in her bag and she used
it along with her pick-axe to retrieve the Longbow and the Book of
the Dead.

A vague nervousness overcame her as the book touched her fingertips;
she realised time was of the essence and that she needed to return to
where she had left the candelabrum and the Bell.

She had barely taken twenty steps after descending the ladder before a
voice suddenly boomed out:

"So thou thought thou couldst kill me, fool."

To her shock, the Wizard of Yendor stood next to her, looking as alive
as ever.  Even more amazingly he quickly cast a spell and there were
suddenly two Wizards of Yendor in her presence.

Fortunately, they did not last long; one drank a potion which sent him
up through the ceiling, and Stormbringer eagerly tore the other to
shreds.  She realised as he collapsed that this was not the last time
he would be seen.

Quickly she arrived at the vibrating square.  The seven candles she
had placed in the Candelabrum were still there, and she lit them
quickly to give her light in which to work.  The candles gave off
a strange light, not entirely to her surprise.  The Bell was rung,
issuing an unsettling shrill sound, and she opened the Book of the
Dead for the first and last time, reciting the ancient runes.

The subsequent rumbling unveiled a staircase down which SGrunt quickly
descended; the evil and power in the air indicated she was near the
Amulet.  Fortunately her ring of conflict was still going strong,
and the welcoming committee (so to speak) were eager to have at each
other.

As she approached the room within which she could feel the Amulet of
Yendor, a booming voice echoed through the cavern.  But the Wizard did 
not stay long - it appeared he could sense the Amulet, too, and the limited
telepathic vision the Longbow granted her revealed that he was now
assailing the High Priest of Moloch.

She fought her way through the remaining crowd, searching for the entrance
to the resting place of the high altar.  As she found it a series of
surprises turned up.

First, the Wizard summoned some pets to deter SGrunt from reaching the
High Priest first.  Amongst them was a large purple worm, which promptly
swallowed the high priest.  The Wizard quickly snatched up the Amulet
and teleported away.

Moments later a shriek filled the air, and suddenly a demon lord appeared
next to SGrunt, the Amulet firmly grasped in his claws.  By the flail,
she recognised him as Yeenoghu.  Three hits from Stormbringer caused him
to flee as well, and she was forced to fight back to the stairs only to
see Yeenoghu escape.

She was able to beat Yeenoghu to the stairs this time and blocked his
means of escape, pummeling him with Stormbringer until he expired,
and from his dead claws she wrested the Amulet of Yendor.

A sense of power filtered through her, and she realised she needed to
get back to the surface.  Attempting to stash the Amulet in her bag
caused her to realise that she could not possibly contain such power.
She quickly ran upstairs.

Only twice before leaving Gehennom did anything impede her; in both cases
it was a mysterious force blocking her from climbing the stairs and 
flinging her in one case down a dungeon level and in the other a few
stairs away from the upstairs.  She wondered if this had anything to do
with the force that prevented her from teleporting in some areas.

As she climbed back past the castle and up two levels, the Wizard accosted
her again.  He fell quickly to Stormbringer.

Thanks in part to the passages SGrunt had dug well in advance, the
balance of the ascent was uneventful.  She realised that a challenge
awaited her at the top of the stairs, and she quickly checked her
belongings to make sure she was prepared for it.

A voice issued a final challenge as she climbed the stairs, and she
suddenly found herself in a claustrophobic space surrounded by solid
rock.  Worse, the Wizard would appear for what would turn out to be
the last time, in addition to a minotaur, an Elvenking (who was not
going to attack), and several pets the Wizard would shortly summon.
She battled her way through them before thinking about what she was
looking for.

At some length previously, she had read some spellbooks.  She could not
remember the spells now, and even attempting confused her; in one
of these bouts of confusion she thoughtlessly pulled out one of the
scrolls she had written in advance and attempted to read it.  Being
confused, she severely mispronounced the words she had written - but
to her shock she suddenly became aware of the portal that was her
destination.

With the large stash of digging wands she had accrued, SGrunt quickly
dug to the portal.  A gust of wind issued forth as she approached, and
she quickly removed her speed boots and put on the levitation boots
she had retrieved so long ago from the unfortunate statue of Perseus.

Stepping through the portal, she found herself as high up as the clouds
in an absence of gravity.  Several air elementals swooped down on her, but
she was not worried; she could sense the conflict in the air.  She used
the same trick as she had before to locate her destination, and attempted
to float over there.

She was swallowed once by an air elemental and came close to dying, but
Stormbringer prevailed.  She cautiously maneuvered the other denizens
of the plane between them and her before proceeding.

SGrunt soon found herself on a fiery plane; locating the portal yielded
not only a destination but a large number of towers of fire as well.
As the elementals, dragons, and demons battled amongst each other,
she floated over to the portal, virtually unnoticed.

A massive pool of water greeted SGrunt on the other side, in which she was
enclosed in a bubble.  She realised she would not be able to make out
her destination if she attempted to locate it right now - besides which
it would move.  She floated about, avoiding the battling kraken and eels,
until she felt she was close to the portal; she then tried to detect it,
and it turned out to be fairly close.  Before entering the portal, she
put her speed boots back on and hefted a wand of fire.

As she stepped through, she realised she was very close to her ultimate
objective, sensing alarm and hostility in the air.  Divine intervention
yielded a pack of hostile Angels surrounding her - thanks to her magic
ring - but she realised they would not be the significant threat here.
Hefting her wand of fire, she inscribed a magical name on the floor
as she had done previously to prevent the escape of various other
hostile spell-casters and demons.

Sure enough, a master lich and an arch-lich arrived to greet her.  As
she cut them to pieces with Stormbringer they summoned a large amount
of monsters, which would soon prove to be to her advantage.

Making her way through a passage she beheld a fearsome sight - Famine
incarnate.  Fortunately, he was quickly cut to shreds by the horde of
priests, adventurers, and divine beings surrounding him.

She quickly arrived at an altar, and recognised it, disappointingly,
to be that of Venus.  She turned around to attempt to find the altar
of Mars to behold Famine reincarnate.  She managed to place the crowd
between them and quickly raced off to find another altar.

On the way to the next altar, Death fell to the crowd.  As she attempted
to battle towards the altar Death approached her; to her surprise
Stormbringer cut him down in two blows.  She soon broke free of the crowd
and beheld the altar, which appeared to be that of Mercury.

SGrunt came to the realisation that Mars and Mercury were opposites,
and made the decision that Mars had not particularly come to her aid.
With a wry grin, she pulled the helm she had stashed ages ago from her
bag and placed it onto her head.  She sensed Mars' fury and Mercury's
pleasure as the Amulet was offered to Mercury, and a divine choir
carried her off to her destiny...

SGrunt, lawful female elven Ranger



